# The Dockerfile does `COPY . .` into the build stage, so anything not excluded
# here ends up in a build layer. Two reasons that matters: secrets, and weight.
#
# The runtime stage only copies `dist`, so the final image was never going to
# ship a .env — but a build layer holding real credentials is still a layer that
# exists, gets cached, and can be pushed by a differently-configured build.

# Real secrets. .env.example is intentionally NOT excluded — it is the template.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Reinstalled inside the image for the right platform; copying the host's copy
# is both wasteful and wrong (native modules are built for the host).
node_modules
dist

# History and tooling: large, and nothing in the build reads them.
.git
.github
.claude
.agents

# Docs and local scratch — not part of the build.
docs
coverage
*.log

# Deliberately NOT excluding *.md: prompt prose lives in
# src/llm/templates/text/*.md and is a build asset the app refuses to start
# without. Docker's `*.md` would not match a nested path today, but relying on
# that subtlety to keep the prompts is not worth the few kilobytes it saves.
